      "raw_utf8": "      A cancellation-safe clock for nilpotent bilinear\n                         dynamics\n                               Anonymous Authors\n\n\n                                       Abstract\n            We determine the exact time to zero for the bilinear map F (x, y) =\n        (y, x(t + y)) on I 2 , where I = tFq [t]/(tm ), q is any prime power and\n        m ≥ 2. With the truncated valuation normalized by v(0) = m, this\n        time is max{2(m − v(y)), 2(m − v(x)) − 1}. The nonlinear term can\n        cancel the leading term of one register, so a coordinatewise linear-clock\n        argument does not suffice. After separating the two parity chains, the\n        other register gives the sharp deadline even in the cancellation case.\n        Consequently zero is the unique recurrent state, the maximum depth\n        is 2m − 2, and exactly q h states have depth at most h for 0 ≤ h ≤\n        2m − 2. We also evaluate every one-step fibre by an elementary ideal\n        calculation. This inverse mechanism is multiplication under separate\n        input and output translations, not a new inversion method. The clock\n        and depth distribution agree with a linear control, but for m ≥ 3\n        unequal nonempty fibre sizes exclude conjugacy to any finite-group\n        endomorphism. The case m = 2 is precisely the linear boundary.\n\n\n1      The map and the question\nNilpotence forces many finite algebraic systems to lose information, but it\ndoes not determine when both registers of a coupled recurrence vanish. For\nthe map studied here, a leading-term cancellation can erase one register ear-\nlier than a linear model predicts. The exact full-state deadline nevertheless\nagrees with that model. Our purpose is to prove this agreement on the\nfull carrier, including the cancellation locus, and to distinguish it from a\nlinearization of the dynamics.\n    Throughout, q is a prime power, m ≥ 2, and\n\n    R = Fq [t]/(tm ),     I = tR,       F : I 2 −→ I 2 ,   F (x, y) = (y, x(t + y)).\n                                                                                 (1.1)\n\n\n\n                                           1\n\fThe symbol t denotes its residue class in R. For a nonzero element z, let v(z)\nbe the least degree of its nonzero coefficients, and put v(0) = m. Coefficient\nmultiplication and counting give\n    v(zw) = min{m, v(z) + v(w)},            |tr R| = q m−r   (0 ≤ r ≤ m).   (1.2)\nAn element with nonzero constant coefficient is a unit: factoring out that\ncoefficient reduces its inverse to a finite geometric series. In particular,\nv(t + z) = 1 whenever z ∈ t2 R.\n    For s ∈ I 2 , define its depth by τ (s) = min{n ∈ Z≥0 : F n (s) = (0, 0)},\ninitially allowing infinity. A state is recurrent if some positive iterate returns\nto it. We prove that all depths are finite and that\n               τ (x, y) = max{2(m − v(y)), 2(m − v(x)) − 1}.                (1.3)\nThe associated depth count and the full target-fibre count follow from dif-\nferent calculations: the first uses parity chains, the second uses the image\nand kernel of multiplication on I.\n     Endomorphism dynamics provide a useful comparison class: their finite\nstate graphs have structural restrictions coming from the group operation\n[1]. The present map is not assumed to be an endomorphism. Multiplicative\nfeedback also occurs in the complex Fibonacci maps (x, y) 7→ (xy + c, x) [2].\nAt c = 0, interchanging coordinates gives the polynomial (x, y) 7→ (y, xy).\nThis is background for the one-step multiplication relation, not a transfer\nof complex dynamics to the nilpotent ideal in (1.1).\n     The contribution is the cancellation-safe temporal theorem for this ex-\nplicit nonlinear map. Neither the resulting numerical clock shape nor the\ngeneral valuation-contraction method is asserted to be new. We evaluate\nthe one-step inverse completely while explicitly deducting its multiplication\nmechanism in Section 4. Equal depth distributions will not be confused with\nconjugate state graphs.\n\n\n2    The exact clock\nThe recurrence representation isolates the only multiplier at which leading-\nterm cancellation matters.\nLemma 2.1. Set x0 = x, x1 = y and\n                        xn+2 = xn (t + xn+1 )     (n ≥ 0).                  (2.1)\nThen F n (x, y) = (xn , xn+1 ). Every xn belongs to I, and xn ∈ t2 R for n ≥ 2.\nThus v(t + xn ) = 1 for n ≥ 2.\n\n                                        2\n\fProof. The iterate identity follows by induction from (1.1). If two consecu-\ntive terms belong to I, both factors on the right of (2.1) belong to I, so their\nproduct belongs to t2 R ⊆ I. Starting with x, y ∈ I proves both inclusions.\nThe last claim follows from the coefficient of t in t + xn .\n\nTheorem 2.2 (Pointwise clock). For every (x, y) ∈ I 2 , formula (1.3) holds.\nZero is the unique recurrent state. The maximum depth is 2m − 2, attained\nexactly at the states with v(y) = 1.\n\nProof. Write a = v(x) and b = v(y). First suppose b ≥ 2. The initial multi-\nplier t + y and every later multiplier in (2.1) have valuation one. Induction\ntherefore gives, for k ≥ 0,\n\n          v(x2k ) = min{m, a + k},           v(x2k+1 ) = min{m, b + k}.      (2.2)\n\nIf a < m, the last nonzero even-indexed term has index 2(m−a−1); if b < m,\nthe last nonzero odd-indexed term has index 2(m − b − 1) + 1. The first\nzero state occurs one index after the last nonzero term, giving the maximum\nin (1.3). If a = m, the even chain is absent and its displayed contribution\nis −1, which cannot dominate the nonnegative odd-chain contribution. If\nb = m, the odd chain is absent and contributes zero. In particular a = b = m\ngives depth zero.\n    Now suppose b = 1. Cancellation in t + y can only accelerate the even\nchain. Lemma 2.1 and (1.2) imply\n\nv(x2k ) ≥ min{m, a + k} (k ≥ 1),             v(x2k+1 ) = min{m, 1 + k}(k ≥ 0).\n                                                                           (2.3)\nFor the second equality, the first update of the odd chain uses t+x2 ; all such\nadjacent even terms lie in t2 R. Hence x2m−3 ̸= 0 but x2m−2 = x2m−1 = 0,\nproving τ = 2m − 2. Since a ≥ 1, this is exactly (1.3). The argument\nincludes m = 2.\n    Every state reaches the fixed state zero, so no other state is recurrent.\nWhen b ≥ 2, both contributions to (1.3) are at most 2m − 3; when b = 1,\nthe depth is 2m − 2. This proves sharpness and its equality condition.\n\n   For example, (t, −t) has x2 = 0, whereas for k ≥ 1 its iterates are\n\n           F 2k (t, −t) = (0, −tk+1 ),       F 2k+1 (t, −t) = (−tk+1 , 0).   (2.4)\n\nPowers are taken modulo tm . The first zero state is still 2m − 2; an exact\nvaluation increment for both chains would have been false.\n\n\n                                         3\n\fCorollary 2.3 (Depth census). For every integer 0 ≤ h ≤ 2m − 2, exactly\nq h states have depth at most h. Depth zero has one state, and each depth\n1 ≤ h ≤ 2m − 2 has (q − 1)q h−1 states.\n\nProof. The two inequalities in (1.3) give\n\n        τ (x, y) ≤ h   ⇐⇒    v(x) ≥ m − ⌈h/2⌉,        v(y) ≥ m − ⌊h/2⌋.\n\nBoth thresholds lie between 1 and m. Formula (1.2) therefore gives\n\n                              q ⌈h/2⌉ q ⌊h/2⌋ = q h\n\nstates. Subtract the count at h − 1 for the exact positive-depth count.\n\n\n3    Complete one-step fibres\nThe inverse fixes one register and leaves a multiplication equation in I.\nThe final valuation stratum requires the kernel inside I, rather than the\nannihilator in all of R.\n\nTheorem 3.1 (Target fibres). Fix (u, w) ∈ I 2 and set d = min{v(t+u), m−\n1}. The target is reachable if and only if w ∈ td+1 R. If it is reachable and\nx0 is one solution of (t + u)x0 = w in I, its full predecessor set is\n\n                 F −1 ({(u, w)}) = {(x0 + k, u) : k ∈ tm−d R},            (3.1)\n\nand has cardinality q d .\n\nProof. The first target coordinate forces y = u, so the remaining equation\nis (t + u)x = w with x ∈ I. If d ≤ m − 2, write t + u = td e for a unit e.\nMultiplication by e permutes every ideal tr R. Consequently multiplication\nby t + u maps I onto td+1 R and has kernel tm−d R. For w = td+1 w0 , a\nconcrete solution is x0 = te−1 w0 ; choosing another lift of w0 changes this\nsolution only by the kernel. Two solutions differ by a kernel element, and\nadding any kernel element preserves the equation. This proves (3.1) in this\ncase.\n    If d = m − 1, then t + u ∈ tm−1 R and (t + u)I = 0, including the case\nt + u = 0. The only reachable second coordinate is w = 0, and every x ∈ I\nis then a solution. Here td+1 R = 0 and tm−d R = I, so the same formula\napplies. The ideal cardinality in (1.2) gives q d .\n\n\n\n\n                                       4\n\fCorollary 3.2 (Distribution and extreme fibres). For 1 ≤ d ≤ m − 2,\nexactly\n                           (q − 1)q 2(m−d−1)                   (3.2)\ntargets have q d predecessors. Exactly q targets have the largest fibre size\nq m−1 ; they are\n                        (−t + ctm−1 , 0),   c ∈ Fq .                   (3.3)\nThe image size is\n                                                   m−2\n                                                   X\n                          | im F | = q + (q − 1)         q 2j .                  (3.4)\n                                                   j=1\n\nAll remaining targets have empty fibres. An empty sum is zero.\n\nProof. Translation u 7→ t + u permutes I. For each ordinary stratum 1 ≤\nd ≤ m − 2, exactly (q − 1)q m−d−1 values of u give valuation d. For each\nsuch u, there are q m−d−1 admissible values of w. Their product is (3.2).\nThe saturated condition t + u ∈ tm−1 R instead has the q solutions in (3.3),\neach permitting only w = 0. These fibres are larger than all ordinary ones.\nSumming their counts gives (3.4). At m = 2 there is no ordinary stratum:\nall q reachable targets have q predecessors.\n\n\n4    Multiplication and linear controls\nThe fibre theorem is an evaluated multiplication relation. On the same\ncarrier I 2 , define\n\n    M (a, b) = (b, ab),     P (x, y) = (x, y + t),           Q(u, w) = (u − t, w).\n\nBoth P and Q are bijections, and direct substitution gives\n\n                                F = Q ◦ M ◦ P.                                   (4.1)\n\nThus every one-step fibre and image count transfers from multiplication. No\nnew inverse mechanism is claimed. Since Q ̸= P −1 , this two-sided identity\nis not an iterate conjugacy and does not supply the temporal proof.\n\nProposition 4.1 (Same clock without endomorphism conjugacy). The lin-\near map L : I 2 → I 2 , L(x, y) = (y, tx), has the same pointwise clock and\ndepth census as F . If m = 2, then F = L. If m ≥ 3, F is not bijectively\nconjugate to any endomorphism of a finite group.\n\n\n\n                                        5\n\fProof. For k ≥ 0,\n\n            L2k (x, y) = (tk x, tk y),   L2k+1 (x, y) = (tk y, tk+1 x).\n\nThese formulas give (1.3) and hence Corollary 2.3. When m = 2, every\nproduct xy with x, y ∈ I vanishes, so F = L.\n    For m ≥ 3, Corollary 3.2 supplies nonempty fibres of distinct sizes q\nand q m−1 . If ϕ : G → G is a finite-group endomorphism and ϕ(g) = h,\nthen ϕ−1 ({h}) = g ker ϕ: applying ϕ to g −1 z proves both inclusions. All\nits nonempty fibres therefore have the same size. A bijective conjugacy\npreserves fibre cardinalities, giving the contradiction.\n\n    The obstruction concerns endomorphisms, not arbitrary nonlinear fac-\ntors. Equal clocks and equal depth distributions are therefore weaker than\na conjugacy in this family; their common numerical shape is not itself the\nadvance of Theorem 2.2.\n\n\n5    Verification scope and limitations\nThe proofs above hold for every prime power q and every m ≥ 2. The\naccompanying author verifier was run for q ∈ {2, 3, 4} and m ∈ {2, 3, 4}.\nIt exhaustively checked every state, its literal transition and orbit-derived\ndepth, and the full predecessor set of every target against the displayed\nformulas. For q = 4 the coefficient field is F2 [α]/(α2 + α + 1), not arithmetic\nmodulo four. The nine carriers contain 5,271 states, and the complete output\ncontains 10,646 records. Two further strict runs reproduced the canonical\noutput byte for byte. These finite checks do not establish the all-parameter\nstatements.\n    The result is specific to (1.1) on the full ideal square I 2 . It establishes\nneither an arbitrary-chain-ring extension nor all-time fibre formulas or a\nclassification of functional-graph isomorphisms. The temporal conclusion is\nnarrower: leading-term cancellation changes one register’s history without\nchanging the exact deadline of the full nonlinear state.\n\n\nReferences\n[1] Alexander Bors. On the dynamics of endomorphisms of finite groups.\n    Applicable Algebra in Engineering, Communication and Computing,\n    28(3):205–214, 2017.\n\n\n\n                                         6\n\f[2] El Houcein El Abdalaoui, Sylvain Bonnot, Ali Messaoudi, and Olivier\n    Sester. On the Fibonacci complex dynamical systems. Discrete and\n    Continuous Dynamical Systems, 36(5):2449–2471, 2016.\n\n\n\n\n                                  7\n\f"
    },
