      "path": "source_only/main.tex",
      "bytes": 473,
      "sha256": "fd308d1a7ef1b583df524b1ab3d161900af2f02895801eff112465a180a71ada",
      "raw_utf8": "\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n\\input{math_commands}\n\\title{A cancellation-safe clock for nilpotent bilinear dynamics}\n\\author{Anonymous Authors}\n\\date{}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\input{sections/0_abstract}\n\\end{abstract}\n\\input{sections/1_setup}\n\\input{sections/2_clock}\n\\input{sections/3_fibres}\n\\input{sections/4_controls}\n\\input{sections/5_scope}\n\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\end{document}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/math_commands.tex",
      "bytes": 390,
      "sha256": "bbee9c7403e8b19d27ab1dae1e5655a6727b47f077a5b39c83277303d39290b1",
      "raw_utf8": "\\newcommand{\\Fq}{\\mathbb F_q}\n\\newcommand{\\Nzero}{\\mathbb Z_{\\geq 0}}\n\\DeclareMathOperator{\\im}{im}\n\\DeclareMathOperator{\\kerop}{ker}\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}\n\\theoremstyle{remark}\n\\newtheorem{remark}[theorem]{Remark}\n\\numberwithin{equation}{section}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/references.bib",
      "bytes": 1167,
      "sha256": "bfdd8c47db1df9b09fb084e6f4bcf87d2fbe60e890d5a76ee104ddd3b34b9cae",
      "raw_utf8": "% Primary-source bibliography prepared 2026-09-11.\n% Provenance and disclosed formatting/name normalization: sources/SOURCE_CHECK.md.\n% Bors: publisher-exported issue year 2017, not the DOI export's online year 2016.\n@Article{Bors2017,\n  author  = {Bors, Alexander},\n  title   = {On the dynamics of endomorphisms of finite groups},\n  journal = {Applicable Algebra in Engineering, Communication and Computing},\n  year    = {2017},\n  volume  = {28},\n  number  = {3},\n  pages   = {205--214},\n  doi     = {10.1007/s00200-016-0304-9},\n  url     = {https://doi.org/10.1007/s00200-016-0304-9}\n}\n\n% DOI export obtained directly; first-author surname parsing is normalized\n% to the publisher's byline and running head, and the page dash is TeX-safe.\n@Article{ElAbdalaouiEtAl2016,\n  author  = {El Abdalaoui, El Houcein and Bonnot, Sylvain and Messaoudi, Ali and Sester, Olivier},\n  title   = {On the {Fibonacci} complex dynamical systems},\n  journal = {Discrete and Continuous Dynamical Systems},\n  year    = {2016},\n  volume  = {36},\n  number  = {5},\n  pages   = {2449--2471},\n  doi     = {10.3934/dcds.2016.36.2449},\n  url     = {https://doi.org/10.3934/dcds.2016.36.2449}\n}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/sections/0_abstract.tex",
      "bytes": 1042,
      "sha256": "a3a22f9b6b7aa0a85ceecf26ce0e78d0b43c56cac63c55af8b73e45de9175583",
      "raw_utf8": "We determine the exact time to zero for the bilinear map\n$F(x,y)=(y,x(t+y))$ on $I^2$, where\n$I=t\\mathbb F_q[t]/(t^m)$, $q$ is any prime power and $m\\geq2$.\nWith the truncated valuation normalized by $v(0)=m$, this time is\n$\\max\\{2(m-v(y)),2(m-v(x))-1\\}$.\nThe nonlinear term can cancel the leading term of one register, so a\ncoordinatewise linear-clock argument does not suffice. After separating the\ntwo parity chains, the other register gives the sharp deadline even in the\ncancellation case. Consequently zero is the unique recurrent state, the\nmaximum depth is $2m-2$, and exactly $q^h$ states have depth at most $h$\nfor $0\\leq h\\leq2m-2$.\nWe also evaluate every one-step fibre by an elementary ideal calculation.\nThis inverse mechanism is multiplication under separate input and output\ntranslations, not a new inversion method. The clock and depth distribution\nagree with a linear control, but for $m\\geq3$ unequal nonempty fibre sizes\nexclude conjugacy to any finite-group endomorphism. The case $m=2$ is\nprecisely the linear boundary.\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/sections/1_setup.tex",
      "bytes": 2687,
      "sha256": "2cba5d79f527eb57b2becccccbc6def5d8bae85369f28ce59f1f35e14aa2d7dc",
      "raw_utf8": "\\section{The map and the question}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\nNilpotence forces many finite algebraic systems to lose information, but it\ndoes not determine when both registers of a coupled recurrence vanish.\nFor the map studied here, a leading-term cancellation can erase one\nregister earlier than a linear model predicts. The exact full-state\ndeadline nevertheless agrees with that model. Our purpose is to prove this\nagreement on the full carrier, including the cancellation locus, and to\ndistinguish it from a linearization of the dynamics.\n\nThroughout, $q$ is a prime power, $m\\geq2$, and\n\\begin{equation}\n R=\\Fq[t]/(t^m),\\qquad I=tR,\\qquad\n F:I^2\\longrightarrow I^2,\\quad F(x,y)=(y,x(t+y)).\n \\label{eq:map}\n\\end{equation}\nThe symbol $t$ denotes its residue class in $R$. For a nonzero element\n$z$, let $v(z)$ be the least degree of its nonzero coefficients, and put\n$v(0)=m$. Coefficient multiplication and counting give\n\\begin{equation}\n v(zw)=\\min\\{m,v(z)+v(w)\\},\\qquad\n |t^rR|=q^{m-r}\\quad(0\\leq r\\leq m).\n \\label{eq:ideals}\n\\end{equation}\nAn element with nonzero constant coefficient is a unit: factoring out that\ncoefficient reduces its inverse to a finite geometric series. In particular,\n$v(t+z)=1$ whenever $z\\in t^2R$.\n\nFor $s\\in I^2$, define its depth by\n$\\tau(s)=\\min\\{n\\in\\Nzero:F^n(s)=(0,0)\\}$, initially allowing infinity.\nA state is recurrent if some positive iterate returns to it. We prove that\nall depths are finite and that\n\\begin{equation}\n \\tau(x,y)=\\max\\{2(m-v(y)),\\,2(m-v(x))-1\\}.\n \\label{eq:preview}\n\\end{equation}\nThe associated depth count and the full target-fibre count follow from\ndifferent calculations: the first uses parity chains, the second uses the\nimage and kernel of multiplication on $I$.\n\nEndomorphism dynamics provide a useful comparison class: their finite state\ngraphs have structural restrictions coming from the group operation\n\\cite{Bors2017}. The present map is not assumed to be an endomorphism.\nMultiplicative feedback also occurs in the complex Fibonacci maps\n$(x,y)\\mapsto(xy+c,x)$ \\cite{ElAbdalaouiEtAl2016}. At $c=0$, interchanging\ncoordinates gives the polynomial $(x,y)\\mapsto(y,xy)$. This is background\nfor the one-step multiplication relation, not a transfer of complex dynamics\nto the nilpotent ideal in~\\eqref{eq:map}.\n\nThe contribution is the cancellation-safe temporal theorem for this\nexplicit nonlinear map. Neither the resulting numerical clock shape nor\nthe general valuation-contraction method is asserted to be new. We evaluate\nthe one-step inverse completely while explicitly deducting its multiplication\nmechanism in Section~\\ref{sec:controls}. Equal depth distributions will not\nbe confused with conjugate state graphs.\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/sections/2_clock.tex",
      "bytes": 3598,
      "sha256": "e86ca17884492ead49d2e1db2e182155eeb5cbcaa2ea96d564a3885c05a775e6",
      "raw_utf8": "\\section{The exact clock}\n\\label{sec:clock}\n\nThe recurrence representation isolates the only multiplier at which\nleading-term cancellation matters.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lem:recurrence}\nSet $x_0=x$, $x_1=y$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n x_{n+2}=x_n(t+x_{n+1})\\quad(n\\geq0).\n \\label{eq:recurrence}\n\\end{equation}\nThen $F^n(x,y)=(x_n,x_{n+1})$. Every $x_n$ belongs to $I$, and\n$x_n\\in t^2R$ for $n\\geq2$. Thus $v(t+x_n)=1$ for $n\\geq2$.\n\\end{lemma}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nThe iterate identity follows by induction from~\\eqref{eq:map}.\nIf two consecutive terms belong to $I$, both factors on the right of\n\\eqref{eq:recurrence} belong to $I$, so their product belongs to $t^2R\n\\subseteq I$. Starting with $x,y\\in I$ proves both inclusions. The last\nclaim follows from the coefficient of $t$ in $t+x_n$.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Pointwise clock]\n\\label{thm:clock}\nFor every $(x,y)\\in I^2$, formula~\\eqref{eq:preview} holds. Zero is the\nunique recurrent state. The maximum depth is $2m-2$, attained exactly at\nthe states with $v(y)=1$.\n\\end{theorem}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nWrite $a=v(x)$ and $b=v(y)$. First suppose $b\\geq2$. The initial\nmultiplier $t+y$ and every later multiplier in~\\eqref{eq:recurrence}\nhave valuation one. Induction therefore gives, for $k\\geq0$,\n\\begin{equation}\n v(x_{2k})=\\min\\{m,a+k\\},\\qquad\n v(x_{2k+1})=\\min\\{m,b+k\\}.\n \\label{eq:exactchains}\n\\end{equation}\nIf $a<m$, the last nonzero even-indexed term has index $2(m-a-1)$;\nif $b<m$, the last nonzero odd-indexed term has index $2(m-b-1)+1$.\nThe first zero state occurs one index after the last nonzero term, giving\nthe maximum in~\\eqref{eq:preview}. If $a=m$, the even chain is absent and\nits displayed contribution is $-1$, which cannot dominate the nonnegative\nodd-chain contribution. If $b=m$, the odd chain is absent and contributes\nzero. In particular $a=b=m$ gives depth zero.\n\nNow suppose $b=1$. Cancellation in $t+y$ can only accelerate the even\nchain. Lemma~\\ref{lem:recurrence} and~\\eqref{eq:ideals} imply\n\\begin{equation}\n v(x_{2k})\\geq\\min\\{m,a+k\\}\\quad(k\\geq1),\\qquad\n v(x_{2k+1})=\\min\\{m,1+k\\}\\quad(k\\geq0).\n \\label{eq:cancellationchains}\n\\end{equation}\nFor the second equality, the first update of the odd chain uses $t+x_2$;\nall such adjacent even terms lie in $t^2R$. Hence $x_{2m-3}\\ne0$ but\n$x_{2m-2}=x_{2m-1}=0$, proving $\\tau=2m-2$. Since $a\\geq1$, this is\nexactly~\\eqref{eq:preview}. The argument includes $m=2$.\n\nEvery state reaches the fixed state zero, so no other state is recurrent.\nWhen $b\\geq2$, both contributions to~\\eqref{eq:preview} are at most\n$2m-3$; when $b=1$, the depth is $2m-2$. This proves sharpness and its\nequality condition.\n\\end{proof}\n\nFor example, $(t,-t)$ has $x_2=0$, whereas for $k\\geq1$ its iterates are\n\\begin{equation}\n F^{2k}(t,-t)=(0,-t^{k+1}),\\qquad\n F^{2k+1}(t,-t)=(-t^{k+1},0).\n \\label{eq:example}\n\\end{equation}\nPowers are taken modulo $t^m$. The first zero state is still $2m-2$;\nan exact valuation increment for both chains would have been false.\n\n\\begin{corollary}[Depth census]\n\\label{cor:depth}\nFor every integer $0\\leq h\\leq2m-2$, exactly $q^h$ states have depth at\nmost $h$. Depth zero has one state, and each depth $1\\leq h\\leq2m-2$ has\n$(q-1)q^{h-1}$ states.\n\\end{corollary}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nThe two inequalities in~\\eqref{eq:preview} give\n\\[\n \\tau(x,y)\\leq h\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n v(x)\\geq m-\\lceil h/2\\rceil,\\quad\n v(y)\\geq m-\\lfloor h/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\nBoth thresholds lie between $1$ and $m$. Formula~\\eqref{eq:ideals}\ntherefore gives\n\\[\n q^{\\lceil h/2\\rceil}q^{\\lfloor h/2\\rfloor}=q^h\n\\]\nstates. Subtract the count at $h-1$ for the exact positive-depth count.\n\\end{proof}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/sections/3_fibres.tex",
      "bytes": 2653,
      "sha256": "8f7150a26eb72fc10fdc9565be9f1a65ae93ccb7e94de068f8ef9990244fad75",
      "raw_utf8": "\\section{Complete one-step fibres}\n\\label{sec:fibres}\n\nThe inverse fixes one register and leaves a multiplication equation in\n$I$. The final valuation stratum requires the kernel inside $I$, rather\nthan the annihilator in all of $R$.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Target fibres]\n\\label{thm:fibre}\nFix $(u,w)\\in I^2$ and set $d=\\min\\{v(t+u),m-1\\}$. The target is\nreachable if and only if $w\\in t^{d+1}R$. If it is reachable and $x_0$\nis one solution of $(t+u)x_0=w$ in $I$, its full predecessor set is\n\\begin{equation}\n F^{-1}(\\{(u,w)\\})=\n \\{(x_0+k,u):k\\in t^{m-d}R\\},\n \\label{eq:fibre}\n\\end{equation}\nand has cardinality $q^d$.\n\\end{theorem}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nThe first target coordinate forces $y=u$, so the remaining equation is\n$(t+u)x=w$ with $x\\in I$. If $d\\leq m-2$, write $t+u=t^d e$ for a\nunit $e$. Multiplication by $e$ permutes every ideal $t^rR$. Consequently\nmultiplication by $t+u$ maps $I$ onto $t^{d+1}R$ and has kernel\n$t^{m-d}R$. For $w=t^{d+1}w_0$, a concrete solution is $x_0=te^{-1}w_0$;\nchoosing another lift of $w_0$ changes this solution only by the kernel.\nTwo solutions differ by a kernel element, and adding any kernel element\npreserves the equation. This proves~\\eqref{eq:fibre} in this case.\n\nIf $d=m-1$, then $t+u\\in t^{m-1}R$ and $(t+u)I=0$, including the case\n$t+u=0$. The only reachable second coordinate is $w=0$, and every $x\\in I$\nis then a solution. Here $t^{d+1}R=0$ and $t^{m-d}R=I$, so the same\nformula applies. The ideal cardinality in~\\eqref{eq:ideals} gives $q^d$.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{corollary}[Distribution and extreme fibres]\n\\label{cor:fibrecensus}\nFor $1\\leq d\\leq m-2$, exactly\n\\begin{equation}\n (q-1)q^{2(m-d-1)}\n \\label{eq:ordinarycount}\n\\end{equation}\ntargets have $q^d$ predecessors. Exactly $q$ targets have the largest\nfibre size $q^{m-1}$; they are\n\\begin{equation}\n (-t+c t^{m-1},0),\\qquad c\\in\\Fq.\n \\label{eq:maximizers}\n\\end{equation}\nThe image size is\n\\begin{equation}\n |\\im F|=q+(q-1)\\sum_{j=1}^{m-2}q^{2j}.\n \\label{eq:image}\n\\end{equation}\nAll remaining targets have empty fibres. An empty sum is zero.\n\\end{corollary}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nTranslation $u\\mapsto t+u$ permutes $I$. For each ordinary stratum\n$1\\leq d\\leq m-2$, exactly $(q-1)q^{m-d-1}$ values of $u$ give valuation\n$d$. For each such $u$, there are $q^{m-d-1}$ admissible values of $w$.\nTheir product is~\\eqref{eq:ordinarycount}. The saturated condition\n$t+u\\in t^{m-1}R$ instead has the $q$ solutions in~\\eqref{eq:maximizers},\neach permitting only $w=0$. These fibres are larger than all ordinary\nones. Summing their counts gives~\\eqref{eq:image}. At $m=2$ there is no\nordinary stratum: all $q$ reachable targets have $q$ predecessors.\n\\end{proof}\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/sections/4_controls.tex",
      "bytes": 1800,
      "sha256": "80b0d26505e30a656ae5f4de25f636dcac64a2d791abd0eabc3d871be92c7256",
      "raw_utf8": "\\section{Multiplication and linear controls}\n\\label{sec:controls}\n\nThe fibre theorem is an evaluated multiplication relation. On the same\ncarrier $I^2$, define\n\\[\n M(a,b)=(b,ab),\\qquad P(x,y)=(x,y+t),\\qquad Q(u,w)=(u-t,w).\n\\]\nBoth $P$ and $Q$ are bijections, and direct substitution gives\n\\begin{equation}\n F=Q\\circ M\\circ P.\n \\label{eq:adapter}\n\\end{equation}\nThus every one-step fibre and image count transfers from multiplication.\nNo new inverse mechanism is claimed. Since $Q\\ne P^{-1}$, this two-sided\nidentity is not an iterate conjugacy and does not supply the temporal proof.\n\n\\begin{proposition}[Same clock without endomorphism conjugacy]\n\\label{prop:linear}\nThe linear map $L:I^2\\to I^2$, $L(x,y)=(y,tx)$, has the same pointwise\nclock and depth census as $F$. If $m=2$, then $F=L$. If $m\\geq3$, $F$\nis not bijectively conjugate to any endomorphism of a finite group.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{proof}\nFor $k\\geq0$,\n\\[\n L^{2k}(x,y)=(t^kx,t^ky),\\qquad\n L^{2k+1}(x,y)=(t^ky,t^{k+1}x).\n\\]\nThese formulas give~\\eqref{eq:preview} and hence Corollary~\\ref{cor:depth}.\nWhen $m=2$, every product $xy$ with $x,y\\in I$ vanishes, so $F=L$.\n\nFor $m\\geq3$, Corollary~\\ref{cor:fibrecensus} supplies nonempty fibres of\ndistinct sizes $q$ and $q^{m-1}$. If $\\phi:G\\to G$ is a finite-group\nendomorphism and $\\phi(g)=h$, then $\\phi^{-1}(\\{h\\})=g\\kerop\\phi$:\napplying $\\phi$ to $g^{-1}z$ proves both inclusions. All its nonempty\nfibres therefore have the same size. A bijective conjugacy preserves fibre\ncardinalities, giving the contradiction.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThe obstruction concerns endomorphisms, not arbitrary nonlinear factors.\nEqual clocks and equal depth distributions are therefore weaker than a\nconjugacy in this family; their common numerical shape is not itself the\nadvance of Theorem~\\ref{thm:clock}.\n"
    },
    {
      "path": "source_only/sections/5_scope.tex",
      "bytes": 1081,
      "sha256": "74218cd9bf444a48f7d5b4f9f683fce455da0801a553be449042924ab4e1bb41",
      "raw_utf8": "\\section{Verification scope and limitations}\n\\label{sec:scope}\n\nThe proofs above hold for every prime power $q$ and every $m\\geq2$.\nThe accompanying author verifier was run for\n$q\\in\\{2,3,4\\}$ and $m\\in\\{2,3,4\\}$. It exhaustively checked every\nstate, its literal transition and orbit-derived depth, and the full\npredecessor set of every target against the displayed formulas.\nFor $q=4$ the coefficient field is\n$\\mathbb F_2[\\alpha]/(\\alpha^2+\\alpha+1)$, not arithmetic modulo four.\nThe nine carriers contain 5,271 states, and the complete output contains\n10,646 records. Two further strict runs reproduced the canonical output\nbyte for byte. These finite checks do not establish the all-parameter\nstatements.\n\nThe result is specific to~\\eqref{eq:map} on the full ideal square $I^2$.\nIt establishes neither an arbitrary-chain-ring extension nor all-time fibre\nformulas or a classification of functional-graph isomorphisms. The temporal\nconclusion is narrower: leading-term cancellation changes one register's\nhistory without changing the exact deadline of the full nonlinear state.\n"
    }
  ]
}
